1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:04,730
These laboratory scenes illustrate the tools and procedures involved.

2
00:00:04,680 --> 00:00:08,085
They are not recordings of Google's experiments.

3
00:00:08,085 --> 00:00:17,537
On October sixteenth, Google Research presented three projects exploring how artificial intelligence could become a more useful partner at the laboratory bench.

4
00:00:17,537 --> 00:00:26,733
The connection between them is experimental feedback: propose an idea, test it, examine what actually happened, and revise the next attempt.

5
00:00:26,733 --> 00:00:29,029
That sounds straightforward.

6
00:00:29,029 --> 00:00:32,983
But a laboratory is much less forgiving than a text window.

7
00:00:32,983 --> 00:00:38,736
A computer program can be perfectly valid and still tell a robot to do something impossible.

8
00:00:38,736 --> 00:00:41,797
A pipette might not fit into a container.

9
00:00:41,797 --> 00:00:43,902
A well might be empty.

10
00:00:43,902 --> 00:00:48,940
Or a liquid might not mix as expected, quietly distorting the result.

11
00:00:48,940 --> 00:00:53,965
Google's first project, called VERID, is designed to bridge that gap.

12
00:00:53,965 --> 00:00:59,858
It keeps a live digital record of the equipment, the liquids and the available pipette tips.

13
00:00:59,858 --> 00:01:04,590
Before commands reach the robot, they pass through a simulated run.

14
00:01:04,590 --> 00:01:09,182
The system checks physical constraints and tracks what has already happened.

15
00:01:09,182 --> 00:01:14,195
The team's demonstrations used dyes and water to test these foundations.

16
00:01:14,195 --> 00:01:19,896
In one case, a planned action would not fit the equipment, so the agent had to replan.

17
00:01:19,896 --> 00:01:26,797
In another, saved state helped it recover after a measurement device failed, without repeating the liquid transfers.

18
00:01:26,797 --> 00:01:30,049
These are useful examples of error handling.

19
00:01:30,049 --> 00:01:33,838
They do not show that every possible mistake can be prevented.

20
00:01:33,838 --> 00:01:40,789
The second project asks a different question: can experimental results help an agent design better molecules?

21
00:01:40,789 --> 00:01:48,277
The researchers worked with aptamers, short strands of DNA that can fold into shapes capable of binding to proteins.

22
00:01:48,277 --> 00:01:54,947
Across three rounds of design and testing, agents received laboratory measurements and revised their candidates.

23
00:01:54,947 --> 00:01:58,072
One attempt exposed a revealing problem.

24
00:01:58,072 --> 00:02:02,588
The molecules appeared to bind strongly, but many were simply sticky.

25
00:02:02,588 --> 00:02:07,103
They attached to unwanted targets and even to the beads used in the test.

26
00:02:07,103 --> 00:02:11,695
Chasing the strongest-looking signal had led the search in the wrong direction.

27
00:02:11,695 --> 00:02:18,021
With those measurements, the agents changed their design strategy and improved the next round's selectivity.

28
00:02:18,021 --> 00:02:24,527
The agent setup also changed between rounds, so this was not a controlled test of feedback alone.

29
00:02:24,527 --> 00:02:31,886
A third project explored protein design, combining human guidance with agent-driven search and laboratory checks.

30
00:02:31,886 --> 00:02:36,274
It produced binders for a protein associated with solid tumours.

31
00:02:36,274 --> 00:02:40,904
Binding a target, however, is only an early research step.

32
00:02:40,904 --> 00:02:44,858
It does not demonstrate a safe or effective cancer treatment.

33
00:02:44,858 --> 00:02:46,899
There are other limits.

34
00:02:46,899 --> 00:02:50,968
These are results reported by the team that built the systems.

35
00:02:50,968 --> 00:02:56,504
The projects are separate building blocks, and bringing them together remains future work.

36
00:02:56,504 --> 00:03:02,575
People still approve molecular designs, check protocols and supervise robot runs.

37
00:03:02,575 --> 00:03:06,682
For viewers, the important change is the direction of travel.

38
00:03:06,682 --> 00:03:13,417
AI research is moving beyond generating plausible answers towards testing ideas against the physical world.

39
00:03:13,417 --> 00:03:17,205
That could help scientists explore more possibilities.

40
00:03:17,205 --> 00:03:21,924
But the useful measure of progress is not how confidently an agent speaks.

41
00:03:21,924 --> 00:03:26,376
It is whether a carefully checked experiment supports what it claims.

